 of the components is $b.$ So, we first draw the vector $b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/78/78afd350dba16899e827e5e8a70cc42512d2466440495ef192f93b2b29357540.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =b-3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+b=-3a+b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52f27c57-6e11-56bc-9c31-145871e25550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515719} {:answer/id #uuid "5ca86c3f-6682-5ebd-994f-73db90ea5e0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515720} {:answer/id #uuid "75c889ff-833b-5752-a882-570604d73b49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515724} {:answer/id #uuid "c860ecc2-edc7-5f60-b973-d274a09c4a69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515729} {:answer/id #uuid "ec004c24-d148-5708-9571-cd0fcfcecb0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75c889ff-833b-5752-a882-570604d73b49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515724} :answer-field/id #uuid "c902e0cf-62a8-5d36-922f-